>self-polymorphing. BESIDES, it is present in the earlier (decent-sized)
>versions, which makes the whole argument void.
>
I don't really see how you could do it.. The self-polymorphing code is not
just one routine that polymorphs you (this is faily small). Most of the code 
is scattered all over, because for lots of your actions the game has to check
for you being polymorphed into another monster (like the shopkeeper addressing
you properly). A quick search through the sources shows, that 
#ifdef POLYSELF appears almost 400 times! You can't really separate that
code into a different file and load it only when needed...

The Quest data are stored in a separate file. What is stored with the game
is just the information about all monsters and all items (thus including
quest specific monsters and items). The additional space used is really
minimal, and I suspect that singling those descriptions out and storing 
them separately from normal monster/item data would actually increase the
size of the code (as you'll need some code to pick them form an external
file and load when needed).

Nethack has indeed a lot of code which almost never executes - 
but that's exactly why I love it. After all those yoears you still see
some new message from time to time. If your machine can't handle it - bad
for you. It probably can't handle much more things, not just nethack...
